  "heat": 3,
  "text": "She asked him for help and he did not answer her. Not one word. She kept going. Matthew fifteen. Jesus goes into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon. And a woman of Canaan comes out after him, crying, \"Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou Son of David. My daughter is grievously vexed with a devil.\" And then the text says something that stops you cold. \"But he answered her not a word.\" Silence. Then the disciples get involved, and not helpfully. \"Send her away, for she crieth after us.\" They are not moved. They are annoyed. Then he says he was sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. She still does not leave. She comes and worships him and says three words. \"Lord, help me.\" And then he gives her the hard line. \"It is not meet to take the children's bread, and to cast it to dogs.\" People argue endlessly about his tone. The text does not record tone, so hold your theory loosely. What is not in dispute is her comeback. \"Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters' table.\" She takes his own metaphor, accepts the terms, and turns it around on him. And he says, \"O woman, great is thy faith.\" Healed that hour.",
